  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records collectively establish temperature-dependent tensile deformation in closely related FCC CoCrFeMnNi alloys and, especially in R3 and R15, connect plasticity to partial dislocations, stacking faults, and local HCP-like structures. R1–R3 also make molecular-dynamics tensile studies of closely related HEA nanowires a clear precedent, while several other records connect temperature, dislocations, and strength. However, none discloses the later paper's specific bulk periodic single-crystal MD design combining crystallographic loading directions, three strain rates, and three temperatures, nor the proposed initiation at vacancy clusters and the particular conclusion of strain-rate-insensitive yield strength. Moving from free-surface nanowires or macroscopic polycrystals to this bulk defect-mediated protocol would require a substantial additional idea, although the literature points in the right direction."
